## Criticisms (criticisms)
[be7c7b257f94e587] (mini.criticism.v1)
Proposal 8f9ab6a16e1d2c1a failed because the proposer misread the rule regarding the priority of code fences. The rule states: "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text" [cfb66510a6ca9655]. 

In the input `"```\n{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}\n```\n{"c": 3}"`, there is a code fence containing objects A and B. Because a fence holds objects, the harness scores the last one inside that fence (object B), ignoring the top-level object C. 

In the rewritten input `"{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}\n{"c": 3}"`, there are no code fences. The harness then falls back to the second part of the rule: "the last top-level object in the text". In this case, object C is the last top-level object. 

However, the execution record [51fe44d44347c47a] shows that for both the input and the rewritten text, the result was `{"c":3}` (the execution log lists `"before": "{"c":3}", "after": "{"c":3}"`). This indicates that the code actually prioritizes the last top-level object regardless of whether a fence exists, or that the fence logic is not behaving as documented. 

Looking at the source [5cf1f38daafcce6b], `recover_json_object` collects `fenced` candidates and `top_level` candidates, then iterates: `for pool in (fenced, top_level): for candidate in reversed(pool):`. This means it checks all fenced candidates (last to first) before checking any top-level candidates. If a fence exists and contains a valid object, it should return that object and never reach the `top_level` pool. 

The failure in execution 8f9ab6a16e1d2c1a is highly unusual: the execution log says the result was `{"c":3}` for the *input* (which had a fence containing B). This suggests the machine is ignoring the fence and taking the last top-level object C, which contradicts the rule "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one". 

This failure matters because it reveals a discrepancy between the documented priority (Fence > Top-level) and the machine's behavior. A proposer should next try to isolate whether the fence is being ignored entirely or if the `top_level` search is somehow overriding the `fenced` search.

